<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Portable Home Assistant integration core for the OpenBurnBar Windows port
    (Phase 4 · integrations lane). This is the DEPENDENCY-FREE half of the HA
    Smart-Display recovery integration: the REST message model, the granular
    response/error mapping, the entity-state -> MediaPlayer projection + Cast
    detection heuristics, the webhook-id CSPRNG-alphabet generator, the recovery
    config + URL normalizer, the blueprint installer (deep link + embedded YAML),
    and the token-store + config-store seams behind injectable interfaces.

    It has NO WinUI / Windows / HttpClient dependency on purpose — the SAME managed
    assembly is:
      * referenced by the Windows integration adapter
        (OpenBurnBar.Integrations.HomeAssistant.Net), which plugs a real
        System.Net.Http transport into the portable HomeAssistantClient, AND
      * unit-tested on the macOS authoring host today via `dotnet test`
        (windows/tests/integrations), driving the client through a FAKE transport
        fed by recorded HA response fixtures — exactly the portable-core pattern
        already proven by OpenBurnBar.Pal.Ipc (VAL-P0-CONPTY-018) and the storage
        seam (VAL-P0-DB-010).

    Parity sources (macOS):
      * AgentLens/Services/HomeAssistant/HomeAssistantClient.swift
          -> Rest/HomeAssistantClient.cs + Rest/HomeAssistantResponseMapper.cs
             + Rest/HomeAssistantStateProjection.cs + Models/*.cs
      * AgentLens/Services/HomeAssistant/HomeAssistantConfig.swift
          -> HomeAssistantConfig.cs + HomeAssistantUrlNormalizer.cs
      * AgentLens/Services/HomeAssistant/HomeAssistantConfigStore.swift
          -> Stores/HomeAssistantConfigStore.cs (+ IKeyValueStore seam)
      * AgentLens/Services/HomeAssistant/HomeAssistantTokenStore.swift
          -> Stores/HomeAssistantTokenStore.cs (ISecretStore seam)
      * AgentLens/Services/HomeAssistant/HomeAssistantWebhookID.swift
          -> HomeAssistantWebhookId.cs
      * AgentLens/Services/HomeAssistant/HomeAssistantBlueprintInstaller.swift
          -> HomeAssistantBlueprintInstaller.cs

    Targets net8.0 so a net8.0-windows WinUI app can reference it, while the
    net10.0 adapter + test projects reference it too (a lower TFM is consumable by
    a higher one). System.Text.Json is in-box; zero external package references.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <RootNamespace>OpenBurnBar.Integrations.HomeAssistant</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>OpenBurnBar.Integrations.HomeAssistant</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
